Social & Environmental
Nausea/vomiting prodrome, Cutaneous erythema/desquamation, Mucosal bleeding/petechiae, Severe lymphopenia

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: SEV-037
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of severe gastrointestinal symptoms and progressive skin changes following an industrial radiography mishap.
Location: Private residence (worker sent home earlier in the day)
Present at Visit: Patient, spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an industrial worker who developed sudden, intractable vomiting, severe fatigue, and a burning red rash on their chest hours after a documented malfunction with a high-activity industrial radiography source. Urgent assessment was required to evaluate for Acute Radiation Syndrome (ARS) and prevent severe dehydration or cardiovascular collapse prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I can't stop throwing up, I'm completely exhausted, and the skin on my chest feels like it's on fire."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a sudden onset of severe nausea, vomiting, and anorexia starting approximately 3 hours after a recognized incident at their industrial workplace (handling a damaged radiography camera). The patient complains of painful, burning cutaneous erythema rapidly developing over the anterior chest (the site closest to the source). There is profound, progressive fatigue and systemic weakness. The patient describes severe abdominal cramps and one episode of non-bloody diarrhea. The spouse notes the patient looks exceedingly pale and is unable to sit up due to dizziness.
Functional Status: Normally independent; currently acutely bedbound by profound weakness and vomiting
Social/Safety Concerns: High likelihood of a massive, potentially lethal absorbed dose of ionizing radiation, severe physiological shock

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 95/60 | HR: 125 | Temp: 37.8 | RR: 24 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Pale, lethargic, actively retching, visibly exhausted, clutching an emesis basin
Physical Exam: Skin examination of the anterior chest reveals a sharply demarcated, intensely erythematous plaque (resembling a severe, deep sunburn) that is exquisitely tender to palpation, indicating acute radiation dermatitis. No blistering or desquamation is present yet, but it is expected. Oral mucosa is intact but dry. Cardiovascular exam reveals marked sinus tachycardia and orthostatic hypotension. Diagnostic tetrad features are highly suspected/present: rapid nausea/vomiting prodrome, cutaneous erythema, and a high probability of impending mucosal bleeding/petechiae and severe lymphopenia pending serial blood counts.
Environmental Observations: Patient is resting in a dark room; multiple basins of vomit are present. The patient is not radioactive (external exposure only, no internal contamination).
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Radiation exposure (Acute Radiation Syndrome - Prodromal phase) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials: 1. Acute Viral Gastroenteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of the sharply demarcated, burning cutaneous erythema localized to the exact site of the industrial exposure, and the sudden, violent onset of the systemic symptoms.
2. Chemical Contact Burn - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The profound, systemic nature of the illness (intractable vomiting, severe fatigue, hypotension) which points to whole-body irradiation rather than an isolated, superficial chemical burn.
3. Sepsis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The specific, undeniable occupational history of a massive radiation source malfunction earlier that day.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the classic, severe prodromal phase of Acute Radiation Syndrome (ARS) following a documented high-dose industrial exposure, characterized by rapid-onset intractable emesis, profound fatigue, and localized cutaneous erythema. Delaying assessment would result in severe hypovolemic shock from fluid loss and delay critical serial hematological monitoring. The objective findings of tachycardia, hypotension, and the pathognomonic early radiation burn completely justify the urgent after-hours MBS intervention to provide immediate fluid resuscitation, potent antiemetics, and coordinate urgent transfer to a specialized tertiary center.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate administration of potent intravenous antiemetics (e.g., Ondansetron) to halt the intractable vomiting. Establishment of IV access for rapid fluid resuscitation (Normal Saline bolus). Urgent dispatch of an ambulance for transfer to a tertiary hospital capable of managing ARS.
Positive Behavioral Change: By accepting this IV fluid and medication immediately, you stop the dangerous dehydration, which stabilizes your heart and blood pressure so you can safely endure the transport to the specialist hospital.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Mandatory notification to the national radiation protection authority and public health department regarding the industrial accident.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover to Emergency Department and Radiation Oncology/Hematology teams. Serial absolute lymphocyte counts (ALC) are critical for dose estimation.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate priority transport to the nearest major tertiary hospital. Monitor airway constantly if vomiting persists during transport. The patient will require isolation due to impending profound, life-threatening neutropenia.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The extreme vomiting is the first phase of radiation sickness. After this passes, you may feel better for a few days (latent phase), but you must remain in the hospital because your bone marrow will temporarily stop producing immune cells, placing you at extreme risk of infection.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to walk. The ambulance is arriving. We will manage your nausea through the IV line so you do not have to swallow anything.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The massive energy from the radiation source has damaged the rapidly dividing cells in your stomach lining, causing the vomiting, and burned your skin. The IV drip replaces the lost fluids directly into your veins.
Actionable Steps: Rest completely. Answer the paramedics' questions about exactly how close you were to the machine, as this helps the hospital calculate the dose you received and plan your bone marrow treatments.

